\section{Modes}{

What differs between surveillance systems is only which resolutions get
recorded, and all three possibilities share one likelihood:\tabular{llll}{
    \tab \code{retraction_only} \tab \code{confirmation_only} \tab \code{both} \cr
   outcomes recorded \tab the negatives \tab the positives \tab both signs \cr
   a missing outcome means \tab not retracted \strong{yet} \tab not confirmed \strong{yet} \tab not resolved \strong{yet} \cr
   target \tab reports never retracted \tab reports eventually confirmed \tab reports resolving positive \cr
   lag support \tab \verb{\{1, 2, ...\}} \tab \verb{\{0, 1, ...\}} \tab \verb{\{0, 1, ...\}} \cr
}


The lag support differs only because a \emph{retraction} in the same period as its
report describes a case never visible in any data vintage, whereas a test
coming back the day it was ordered is ordinary.

In \code{mode = "both"}, \code{revision_delay} is one shared law for positive and
negative resolutions. This first prototype deliberately does not fit separate
competing-risk lag laws. In a one-outcome mode, the same argument denotes the
lag for the outcome that is recorded: confirmation or retraction respectively.

\code{"auto"} reads \code{unique(revision_type)} over the \strong{full} data, not the as-of
view, so the mode is a stable property of the data source and does not flip
between backtest dates.
